Hi,
I initially blamed this on hald but am pretty sure now udev is
the real culprit causing USB resets and wedging the HP r707 camera.
Stopping udev daemon allows me to treat the HP r707 like a
normal USB storage device (mount using static /dev/sd* and
copy files to/from the camera). I need help interpreting
the usbmon output below.

Since I'm running ubuntu on this laptop, I filed a bug with ubuntu:
    https://bugs.launchpad.net/ubuntu/+source/hal/+bug/180472

"/lib/udev/vol_id" is either causing the reset or a victim of trying
to talk to a hung device:

grundler@mb500:~$ ps -ef | fgrep udev
root      1795     1  0 Jan18 ?        00:00:00 /sbin/udevd --daemon
root      6004  1795  0 09:33 ?        00:00:00 /sbin/udevd --daemon
nobody    6015  6004  0 09:33 ?        00:00:00 /lib/udev/vol_id --export /dev/.tmp-8-0
grundler  6034  5792  0 09:33 pts/1    00:00:00 grep -F udev

I have a usbmon trace for 2.6.23.13 (appended) along with dmesg output.
What I need is someone to interpret this trace, tell me what happened,
then I can work on tracking down "vol_id"'s role in this mess.

Documentation/usb/usbmon.txt explains bits of what the data mean,
but I have no documentation and little experience with USB protocol.
I need a "USBmon" type app to explain when device responds with an error,
invalid data, or times out. Help?

Lastly, I updated the HP r707 firmware from 2.00.23 to 2.00.24 and both
versions of firmware exhibit the same behavior.

thanks in advance,
grant


